  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Sierra Nevada's federal contract obligations are $2B for FY2026 to date. Sierra Nevada has 43 awards this fiscal year, and the average award size is $46.1M. Obligations are down 11.6% year over year. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $2B, while the Department of Homeland Security obligated $110K. Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest market for Sierra Nevada with $1.5B in obligations, followed by All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services at $398M and Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $115M. Recent fiscal-year history shows rising obligations from FY21 through FY25 with FY21 at $112M, FY22 at $539M, FY23 at $699M, FY24 at $1.3B, and FY25 at $2.2B. Current-year totals are partial. The figures here are contract obligations except for award-value totals in the largest-awards and expiring-contracts tables, which are total contract values. This snapshot highlights where federal dollars are concentrated by agency and NAICS market for Sierra Nevada through FY2026 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who buys from Sierra Nevada?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $2B from Sierra Nevada in FY2026 to date. The Department of Homeland Security obligated $110K. Other buying agencies are not listed in the top agencies table for FY2026 to date. Fiscal-year history shows growing DoD-directed obligations since FY21, peaking at $2.2B in FY25."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What does Sierra Nevada sell to the government?",
        "body": "Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest industry for Sierra Nevada with $1.5B in obligations. Sierra Nevada also sells professional and technical services valued at $398M and systems and instrument manufacturing valued at $115M. Smaller markets include Computer Systems Design Services at $5.4M, telecommunications at $3.8M, and research services at $2.7M."
      }
    ]
